 Wrapping obligation: the ten permanent primitives (listFiles, searchText, searchIdentifiers, readLines, readAround, listSymbols, findDefinitions, findReferences, getImports, getGitDiff) are NOT exposed as direct tools — they are only callable inside a function_try source. Wrap the primitives a question needs in one TypeScript function expression with typed inputs and try it with function_try(source, params); direct primitive calls are impossible in this mode. Use a TypeScript function expression with typed inputs; the harness passes params to it. searchText(query) returns {file,line,text}[], readLines(file,start,end) returns {file,start,lines}, and listFiles() returns paths. Give useful procedures a valid TypeScript identifier and parameterize variations. When a stored procedure fails or is unusable for its declared subject, you may submit a corrected function_try under its existing id only if its purpose and contract remain unchanged; otherwise use a new name. Supply question as a stable canonical question independent of this Tour and a non-empty English description stating what the procedure does, its scope, the shape of its result and its key limits. Every successfully executed function_try trial is automatically submitted to post-Tour selection — no remember flag is needed; the selection step considers actual contribution, correctness, documentation and duplication, and consolidation re-executes the exact code and params and re-checks every citation, so only deterministically validated selections persist. Do not wrap a primitive merely to remember it. Combine independent outputs in your Tour logic; never call one memorized procedure from another. Syntactic matches are candidates, not proven semantic relations; report missing evidence as uncertainty. If a stored function was selected for its declared subject but returned unusable output, call memory_unusable."},{"role":"user","content":"Dans ce workspace, comment fonctionne la réutilisation de la mémoire de proc